&lt;p&gt;# Comments: 1&lt;/p&gt;</description><guid isPermaLink="false">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49699202</guid><pubDate>Mon, 14 Sep 2026 16:04:40 +0000</pubDate></item><item><title>Ask HN: What would make you trust an AI's assessment of your understanding?</title><link>https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49699185</link><description>&lt;p&gt;Creating an AI tutor that won't let you progress without having learned anything — not just completing a test. You train, are diagnosed for any problems in understanding, and there is now a live voice challenge where you need to verbally explain your response to an AI, and then it drills down with additional questions.&lt;p&gt;The voice feature was created by me under the suspicion that it is way more difficult to pretend to have understood something while talking to someone rather than choosing a proper multiple choice answer. But to be honest, I don't know if that is a valuable idea or just another sign of my attachment to the most complicated thing I created.&lt;p&gt;So, the question I want to ask seriously is: What would help you to trust an AI's diagnosis of your understanding? And is it even worthwhile, or should I stick to the solution of doing spaced repetition and trusting myself?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
